<p><font face="Arial" size="4" color="#4E657C">The Fragility of Life</font> </strong><small>Posted Tuesday, November 14, 2000 by <a href="mailto:buur@eqrants.com">Buur</a></small><br><font face="Arial" size="3">With the info I have received from a couple of different sources, I feel pretty confident in saying that the story Lum broke is, unfortunately, true. <br><br>I never knew Sheyla personally. Very few of us did. From what has been said so far we can determine that she had a rough life. Her mother died 4 years ago and she had to go through the stresses of living with a foster family. Then she became a mother herself at the age of 16. She was relieved of her child when she was deemed unfit to be a mother. She was in the midst of a custody battle to get her child back. Not the type of things you would expect to have to deal with at the age of 18. That is the time you should be thinking about graduating high school and having fun with your friends. The real world shouldn't have such a tight grip on you.<br><br>We also have heard and seen that she was a very talented artist. She also was a wonderful singer.<br><br>Unfortunately, to her, the bad outweighed the good and she took her own life last weekend, shortly after being removed from the EQ guide program. Whether the two things are related isn't important. This isn't an excuse to flame Verant and I am hesitant to even mention them in this story. The sorrow in all this is that a young woman saw no hope in her life and rather then fight through it, she ended it.<br><br>I can't explain why this story has touched me so much. I will just say that I am well aware of the fragility of life. I have been down before, very down, but I have always had someone to turn to when all hope seemed lost and all life seemed unimportant. Shelya appears to not have had anyone to turn to, or just chose not to turn to anyone.<br><br>Life is fragile. You may have everything going right for you and no worries in the world. That could all change tomorrow. Maybe the person you sit next to every day but never talk to is going through something similar to what Shelya went through. Perhaps they have no one to turn to and feel lost and that all hope is gone. Next time you see them ask them how they are doing, show that you care a little bit. Something as simple as that could make a world of difference in someone's life. <br><br>Maybe some day they will be there to return the favor.<br><br><a href="http://forums.eqrants.com/showthread.php?threadid=618"><b>Comments.</b></a></p>
