<p><font face="Arial" size="4" color="#4E657C">Gordon Comments on Halloween Massacre</font> </strong><small>Posted Thursday, November 2, 2000 by <a href="mailto:buur@eqrants.com">Buur</a></small><br><font face="Arial" size="3">Gordon decided to swing by Whineplay and leave his <a href="http://http://boards.station.sony.com/everquest/Forum2/HTML/027852.html"><b>two cents</b></a> regarding the 'Halloween Event'.<br><br><font color="#00FF00">I've noticed that there are a bunch of threads on the events last night. Some people enjoyed themselves, others didn't. I think that this is pretty much going to be standard for virtually everything dynamic event that happens in EverQuest. <br>We cannot have a situation where we provide ample warning for everything that is supposed to be a surprise in game. That surprise is a big portion of what makes these events exciting. If we were to start giving explicit warnings before things happen, the target zones would immediatly be innundated with people ready to take part when an event is launched, and the objective of the event would be overcome in very short order.<br><br>Yes, characters die in these types of events. They'll even lose experience if they are killed by an NPC. It's part of the game, and part of what makes EverQuest exciting. <br><br>As mentioned by Bill Coyle, one of our game designers, the characters who die in some of these events are the victims who need to be saved. Whether you are a victim, or one heros who overcome the challenge, you are still part of the story.<br><br>-Gordon</font> <br><br>Once again, Verant proclaims that dying is fun. How is dying in an event that you can't possibly participate in fun? These players didn't ask to be part of this event, they were just victims of another stupid Verant massacre. <br><br>Verant claims the victims are part of the story? What was that story again? Did the dragon raid for a particular reason? If I totally missed the whole plot would someone please let me know. And what exactly do the victims get for their part in the plot? Repeated deaths? They certainly can't kill the dragon, they don't get any of the loot when the uber players show up and kill the dragon. They don't get anything out of it at all. I am not a rocket scientist by any means, nor a game programmer for that matter, but I can almost guarantee I can come up with something a little more fun for all the players then unwanted deaths and poorly thought out 'events'.<br><br>Verant continues to ignore what the players want and insists on telling them how to have fun. Why do I pay for this game again?<br><br><a href="http://forums.eqrants.com/showthread.php?threadid=431"><b>A thread is started on this, feel free to continue to comment.</b></a> </p>
<a name="newsitem973085722,18924,"></a>
<p><font face="Arial" size="4" color="#4E657C">Halloween Event</font> </strong><small>Posted Wednesday, November 1, 2000 by <a href="mailto:buur@eqrants.com">Buur</a></small><br><font face="Arial" size="3">Last night Verant had a <a href="http://boards.station.sony.com/everquest/Forum2/HTML/027691.html"><b>Halloween event</b></a> for the players. It was the typical Verant event. Drop an uber MOB into a zone with unprepared players and watch the carnage. <br><br>In this case they dropped Faydedar, a high level dragon that normally spawns in Timorous Deep, into the Lake of Ill Omen. Of course many players died, died again, cursed at Verant, died again and lost a level. This is what makes EverQuest fun though right? Dying is fun.<br><br>I have no problem with Verant trying to spice things up a bit in game. This game is so ridiculously static that it is almost like playing Pac-Man. Just chomp away at the experience and hope to avoid Blinky, Pinky, Inky and Clyde long enough to level. Rinse and repeat. <br><br>The problem is that in events like this who can have fun? Not the lower levels that are continuously slaughtered. Sure it might be cool for them to see a dragon, but after the 5th death I am sure the uniqueness of it wears out. The only players that can possibly enjoy something like this are the high levels who can get a good group together to engage the MOB. This, of course, leaves the lower levels screwed. Not only were they slaughtered by a MOB they had no chance of standing up to, but they don't get to participate in any of the battle to kill it.<br><br>I guess having an event for the lower levels is a bit much to ask. I mean, what fun is it for Verant unless 50 players die and lose experience. Having a group of 8-14th level MOB's attack a city so that lower level players could have fun just wouldn't fit in with The Vision&#8482; now would it?<br><br><a href="http://forums.eqrants.com/showthread.php?threadid=431"><b>Am I right, am I wrong? Your opinions are welcome.</b></a> </p>
<a name="newsitem973020270,40591,"></a>
