<p><font face="Arial" size="4" color="#4E657C">Think Your Characters Are Safe?</font> </strong><small>Posted Friday, October 20, 2000 by <a href="mailto:buur@eqrants.com">Buur</a></small><br><font face="Arial" size="3">I wanted to mention this yesterday, but my on-again, off-again connection to my site was off. So you can blame day late news on the wild and wacky internet.<br><br>A while back there was a bug in EverQuest that allowed you to duplicate someones character, complete with items. I won't go into the details about it because this is not a bug site and stuff. Anyway, Verant immediately stomped out the bug and all was well...so we were led to believe.<br><br>Apparently <a href="http://boards.station.sony.com/everquest/Forum2/HTML/025115.html"><b>the bug is back</b></a> or possibly it never went away.<br><br>The owner of the account was lucky in this case. If the exploiter hadn't been caught in the act by GM's you know damn well that Verant would have fallen back on their 'The Customer is always wrong because we couldn't possibly have a flaw in <i>our</i> game' policy. Maybe in the future Verant can be a little more trusting of their players when one claims to have lost all his/her items to a bug.<br><br>It's actually pretty scary. A major problem like this somehow manages to slip through QA, even though Verant was aware the problem existed before. You can call it a fluke, or say that it is so hard to repeat that it probably won't happen again. I call it poor all around execution on Verants part. Poor programming, poor QA testing, poor players for repeatingly having to deal with it.<br><br><a href="http://forums.eqrants.com/showthread.php?threadid=286"><b>Minvaren started a thread on this already. Add your own comments.</b></a> </p>
