<p><font face="Arial" size="4" color="#4E657C">EverQuest Toys</font> </strong><small>Posted Friday, October 20, 2000 by <a href="mailto:buur@eqrants.com">Buur</a></small><br><font face="Arial" size="3">I saw over on <a href="http://www.everlore.com"><b>Everlore</b></a> that there has been a press release issued for the new EQ toys that are coming out. Here is a clip from it:<br><br><font color="#FFFF00">"The challenge in creating a line of toys based on a computer game," said Jon Huston, Creative Director for Toy Vault, "Is to produce figures that look like the game characters, but with an added level of realism and extra detail that may not show in the original computer animation. Our EverQuest action figures are not only going to meet that challenge but surpass it. Our ultimate aim," said Huston, "is to bring to the marketplace, the most realistically rendered figures ever based on a popular computer game."</font><br><br>From what I understand these figures will be so realistic that Verant will actually have the ability to nerf them. Imagine this scenario. You and some buddies are playing with your EQ toys. Your Iskar Necromancer figure (I hope I don't get banned for using the work Iskar in a fan fictional sorta way...) is repeatedly kicking the hell out of your buddies Dark Elf Wizard figure. Verant will actually be able to see the results and if they feel like the Iskar Necromancer has too big of an advantage they will remove it's plastic weapons so that the Dark Elf Wizard will be more evenly matched.<br><br>When asked why the Iskar would be nerfed rather then the Dark Elf be buffed, Verant officials said,"It is our Vision&#8482;, our toys and our decision. Go screw yourselves. Oh, and please buy our expansion, The Scars of Velious. We promise it isn't just a giant Everfrost."<br><br><a href="http://forums.eqrants.com/showthread.php?threadid=290"><b>Got Toys?</b></a> </p>
