<p><font face="Arial" size="4" color="#4E657C">I'm back...</font> </strong><small>Posted Monday, September 11, 2000 by <a href="mailto:buur@eqrants.net">Buur</a></small><br><font face="Arial" size="3">I hate to come off as a whiner or a complainer...err...wait a minute, no I don't! This whole site is based on whining and complaining! Having said that, let me whine a bit.<br><br>Moving into this new apartment has been, without a doubt, the most insanely difficult move I have ever been a part of. Nothing went right, and when I say nothing I really mean nothing. It's not like when you say nothing went right and you really mean one or two things got screwed up. Everything involved in this move got screwed up by someone other then myself. <br><br>One of the major utility companies had a problem right away, when we tried to get the electricity turned on my friend was informed that his Social Security number was already being used for electricity, BY SOMEONE ELSE! Yes, someone out there was desperate enough for electricity that they actually used a falsified Social Security number. That situation was eventually cleared up, and put me smack into a dreaded conflict with my insurance company.<br><br>I signed up for renters insurance on August 22nd. I was scheduled to move into my new place on September 8th. By my calendar, and hopefully yours too, that is over two weeks. You can get a lot done in two weeks. Major tennis championships can be played in two weeks. In two weeks time you can camp and receive some k3wl l3wt in Everquest (my pathetic attempt to keep this partially on topic). <br><br>Apparently two weeks is not long enough for a major insurance company to enter your information into their computer though. I called them on the 6th of September requesting that some info on my insurance be faxed to me. The guy I talked to said no problem and assured me I would have it that very afternoon. Well, of course, it never came. I called the guy back the next day to find out that he had decided to take a day off. Great timing asshole. You see, this insurance was mandatory for me to sign my lease; not optional protection for myself. After talking with some people it was determined that my agent just never got around to actually entering my info into their system. After some heated words and a few extended waiting on hold periods, my situation was resolved and I had insurance, 4 hours before I had to sign my lease. Idiots.<br><br>This brings us to the cable company. Comcast, god bless 'em, has some problems. Once again, in an effort to avoid not having services when I moved into the new place, I ordered early. The cable TV was all set and scheduled to turned on the day I moved. Excellent, I can't make it too long without ESPN (Yes, on top of being a computer dork I am also a sports geek. I know all you woman are drooling over me now. I am single, feel free to drop me a line). At the same time I ordered cable TV, I tried to order a cable modem. I was informed that it wouldn't be possible because, get this, they can't enter two orders into their system. Yes, a major cable TV company cannot handle having two separate orders for one customer. This is equivalent to buy.com not being able to send you two things at once.  After asking for an explanation as to why this is so difficult the best answer I could get from a representative was, "It's the software we use, it's crazy." Yes, crazy software is responsible for me being netless at home. Ack...<br><br>My final problem is a real doozy. Actually it isn't my final problem but it is the final one I am going to share with you. I am sure you are bored of my problems by now anyway.<br><br>I rented a UHaul truck to make the move easier. With a 17' truck I could move all my stuff at once and save a lot of time. HAH! I got my truck loaded at 11:00 a.m. the day of the move. The apartment wasn't ready yet so I took a girl I know out to lunch to kill the time. In addition to killing time I also managed to kill my Uhaul truck. Well, I didn't manage to kill it but it managed to just kind of die. By no fault of my own mind you. It just wouldn't start, the battery had died for no apparent reason. I should have known there would be problems when the Uhaul guy told me there was 'a trick' to starting the thing. Generally speaking, when someone tells you there is 'a trick' involved to doing anything it means that something is broke and you are going to have problems.<br><br>I managed to get a jump start and proceeded to finish off my move. By this time it was 6:30 and I was exhausted. My friend called and asked if I would bring the truck by to help him get some stuff, to which I said sure. The truck, apparently more exhausted then I was, once again refused to start. Yep, I spent $80+ on a Uhaul truck that could barely manage to complete one trip.<br><br>At that point I decided to say screw it and I got drunk. Moving sucks people, I highly advise against doing it yourself. Hire people to do it for you. The best work is the work you can get other people to do for you.</p>
