<p><font face="Arial" size="4" color="#4E657C">Patch Rant&#8482;</font> </strong><small>Posted Wednesday, August 30, 2000 by <a href="mailto:buur@eqrants.net">Buur</a></small><br><font face="Arial" size="3">Verant didn't give me much to work with this week. I think I start every Patch Rant&#8482; by saying something really similar to that don't I? Regardless, here we go.<br><br><font color="#FFFF00">*Today's Patch*<br><br>Today we modified the EverQuest client to implement the following changes:<br><br>- Corrected a bug that would cause players to crash if they entered the game, exited to the server selection screen, and tried to reenter the game.</font><br><br>- That's good. I was personally getting a bit tired of logging on, then immediately logging off to see if I could try to relog on, only to crash. It just got tedious.<br><br><font color="#FFFF00">- Corrected a bug that caused delays when switching between UI modes due to the text wrapping change implemented last week.<br>- The /book command (/book #) now accepts pages 1 through 50, and will switch to the proper page.</font><br><br>- Because switching to the wrong page was confusing. The real problem is when you actually switched to the wrong book. I once tried to switch to page 12 of my spellbook, the next thing I knew I was reading 'The Catcher in the Rye'. And no, it isn't about baseball. Yeah, I was disappointed too.<br><br><font color="#FFFF00">- Opening your spellbook will automatically cause you to begin meditating providing that you have the skill.</font><br><br>- If you don't have the skill you will just kinda sit there, you know, not meditating. Makes sense I suppose.<br><br><font color="#FFFF00">- Removed the /meditate command since it is no longer necessary.<br>- You can now activate a hotkey while the spellbook is open by pressing the corresponding number-key.</font><br><br>- Just, whatever you do, don't try an activate a Hot Pocket!!!!!!<br><br><font color="#FFFF00">- The "/target [name]" command can be used to target other players by name. Please note that this ability is subject to the range to the target.</font><br><br>- That would be as opposed to the "/target [name]" command targeting trees. Please note that this ability is subject to the range of the tree.<br><br><font color="#FFFF00">- EverQuest will now write the original default colors when creating the EQCLIENT.INI file, rather than the new colors that were put in a few weeks ago.<br>- The @-character can be used in emotes to indicate a target. For instance, if you target "a gnoll" and type "/emote laughs cruelly at @", the output will be, "Playername laughs cruelly at a gnoll".</font><br><br>- To which the gnoll will reply,"Hey, screw you buddy. I got it bad enough already, I am a frickin' gnoll! You don't have to go around cruelly laughing at me. Asshole!"<br><br><font color="#FFFF00">- People with the /LFG (Looking for Group) flag set will now have their over-head name prefixed with an asterisk. Remember that you can search for people looking for a group by using the "/who" and "/who all" commands. Typing "/who all ranger 40 50 lfg" will return a list of all rangers between 40 and 50 that are looking for a group.</font><br><br>- And people with the new /LFGT (Looking for Good Time) flag set will now have their over-head name prefixed with an exclamation point.<br><br><font color="#FFFF00">- The "Sense" series (Undead, Animal, and Summoned) will no longer point you towards people's pets.</font><br><br>- Thank god. Whenever I cast that spell I find myself drawn toward my neighbors dog.<br><br><font color="#FFFF00">*Note on Voice Recognition*<br><br>Last week we implemented voice recognition technology in EverQuest. To use this new feature, please refer to the EQROKManualSupplement.doc file in your EverQuest directory. Please note that you must already have a voice recognition engine that is compliant with the Microsoft Speech API (SAPI), version 4.0. The voice recognition technology was tested with Dragon NaturallySpeaking(r) v4.0. Check with the developer of your software to find out if it is SAPI 4.0 compliant.</font><br><br>I had been meaing to talk about this anyway. Has their ever been a more blatant case of discrination then this! Sure this helps out those with handicaps that can't use a keyboard and a mouse real well, and that is a noble thing. But what about the blind players? Where are their braille controls? I am sure many of my blind readers would appreciate braille controls in EverQuest! You know how hard it is to navigate when you can't see where you are going?<br><br>And what about those that have a disablity and are mute? Can we get some retinal controls for them? Come on Verant! Get with it! The players won't take your abuse anymore!!<br>(The above was humor. If you were offended by it then too damn bad.)<br><br><font color="#FFFF00">- The EverQuest Team</font> <br>- The EQ Rants Team</p>
