<p><font face="Arial" size="4" color="#4E657C">It Never Ends</font> </strong><small>Posted Thursday, June 22, 2000 by <a href="mailto:buur@eqrants.net">Buur</a></small><br><font face="Arial" size="3">Well, this just refuses to go away. This is *supposedly* follow up  from the guy who wrote the original article at GameFan. I say supposedly because it was posted in the <a href="http://boards.station.sony.com/everquest/Forum4/HTML/054536.html"><b>EQ Bitchfest</b></a>, so there is no way of knowing for sure if it was him or someone playing a little joke. In it he tries to clear up a few things.<br><br><font color="#FFFF00">Hi all. I'm Jasen Torres- the duder who wrote this little article that caused so much trouble.<br><br>I've come to state the following:<br><br>1. Robert was grossly misinformed. The "gift package" was on the test server before kunark was released. I was given a lvl 60 iksar monk to help test out Veeshan's Peak (which was really cool). That char was deleted once Kunark was released.<br><br>2. The article was not pulled because Verant/Sony is a tyrant or because GameFan doesn't back up it's statements. It was taken down because it was an incomplete feature that showed what I was trying to say from a one-sided tilt. Anytime one outs a game or company to the test, they should at the very least include that companies thoughts. That's my fault.<br><br>3. The point of the article (if you read it) wasn't to get my wrongs "right-ed", but to raise the issue of in-game play with our readers. I asked for nothing in retribution other than readers who had similar stories. After looking at the emails I received regarding this, I don't think I would have posted any of these.<br><br>-jasen</font> <br><br>Well there you have it, exciting stuff eh? Hopefully now this story can die the death it deserves.<br><br><a href="http://www.eqrants.net/ubb/Forum2/HTML/000008.html"><b>I think this should be the end of the line for this story.</b></a> </p>
<a name="newsitem961635616,91491,"></a>
<p><font face="Arial" size="4" color="#4E657C">Game Fan Article</font> </strong><small>Posted Wednesday, June 21, 2000 by <a href="mailto:buur@eqrants.net">Buur</a></small><br><font face="Arial" size="3">This article appeared on <a href="http://www.gamefan.com"><b>Game Fan</b></a> a few hours ago. It was promptly pulled down, apparently because of pressure from Verant. The editor didn't want to spoil good relationships with the company so he bowed to their wishes. We here at EQ Rants have no such relationship with Verant so it will be posted for your reading enjoyment.<br><br><font color="#FFFF00">The milk has been spilt, my loyal readers, and you'd better believe I'm going to cry over it. In fact, I hope you EverQuest fans will cry with me, and with your stories we will heal our wounds together...<br><br>Here's my story: I quit one of my all-time favorite games last night because I could no longer take the problems I have with it. I'm one of two resident EverQuest freaks here at GameFan, and it's a huge ordeal when a longtime fan such as myself quits the game; I mean, I'll be the first to say that EverQuest has provided me with many positive experiences. I've been playing it since it came out, and I've enjoyed it much--but I can suffer no more. My problem--and I feel I'm not alone on this one--is with its persistent bugs and the lack of its GMs' desire to sort the situation out. I'm going to give two extremely specific examples that have happened to me in the last month, and while these two in particular are the very reasons I quit the game, I'd like to point out that I've been screwed over many more times than I'll mention here...<br><br>The first time was simply a bug. My mid-level bard sat against a wall near the ocean of the dark elf port town of The Overthere; I played my invisible song to keep safe, I camped, and there was no kind of danger. Then, the next time I logged on, I was on the dock to the boat (which travels to Timorious Deep), next to two guards who are 'kill on site' (KoS), and naturally, I was slashed to death. Yet the point at which I originally camped was so far from the dock, it wasn't even funny... It was a completely obvious bug. There was no way I could've camped at the dock; the bard's invisible song doesn't work on the undead guard that I was right next to, and if I'd been invisible to the undead, I wouldn't have made it past the dark elf guard that helped kill me. Finally, reclaiming my corpse was near impossible--I just died again and again. Thus, I decided to contact a GM. <br><br>Now, GMs are supposed to be helpful; they're supposed to assist the player when the game has wronged him. This experience, however, was different... I waited for an hour to speak to the senior GM (an hour!), and all he did was argue with me. He told me he needed proof if he was to do anything about the bug, so I asked him what proof I could show him. He paused, and then admitted that there was no real proof that I could show him, but still maintained he would still not help me; he wouldn't even summon my corpse to a safe place to loot it. I was infuriated. I explained to him that there's no way I could've logged on at that spot, with an undead guard right next to me. He said, however, that since I had no proof of this bug, he couldn't do anything. And yes, I'm still bitter...<br><br>Case number two happened just last night, and has made me quite the bitter fellow this morning. I had just taken my bard off of the stormbreaker boat in the Ocean of Tears, on Sister Island... Then, a pirate came up to bash me, and I started to run and switched my drum for my two weapons. As I attempted to put the first weapon in its slot, however, I missed, dropped it on the ground (I freaked, because I hate when this happens), and turned around to pick it up. I reached for it, but it disappeared just as I grabbed it. It just plain disappeared! I continued to freak out, killed the pirate with my second weapon, and returned to search the area again. Yet my treasured sword had disappeared, for no good reason... There were only three other people in the zone, a necro on Gargoyle Island and some goblin hunters. I was the only one on Sister Island, so I knew that some invisible character hadn't grabbed it. I felt nothing for the game but utter disdain, and yet my foul experience was not yet over...<br><br>Again I called for a GM, hoping that I would be helped, this time for sure. After all, this<br>was an obvious bug... I explained my quick little story, and he admitted that it was probably a bug, yet he said that he couldn't replace it. So I asked if he could "back up" my character from yesterday, a process GMs do when servers crash or people's corpses disappear. I've been "backed up" before, on another character, when I logged on and<br>suddenly my equipment was all gone, and I hadn't died... But the GM told me he had no good reason to back my character up. I was filled with contempt for the game, and started to argue rudely--upon which the GM argued back, saying I had no proof that I'd lost anything due to a bug. We argued for fifteen minutes (simply handing me the weapon or backing up my character would've taken much less time), and I was driven over the edge... <br><br>So I quit EverQuest. I can no longer stand these horrible bugs that waste hours of my time, nor the lack of GM interest in correcting the situation; it really makes me feel as if they just don't care about the player anymore. Either of these situations could've been corrected easily and quickly, with no harm or unfairness to other players, but instead, the GMs chose to accuse me of fabricating lies and engaging in arguments. This is utterly ridiculous... The main reason we pay the game's subscription fee is for servers and<br>service--and I'll be the first to tell you that the server situation is horrible; it's fraught<br>with overcrowding and crashes. And now that these incidents have happened, I question why people pay at all... My personal experience has been tainted by these factors, and as a result, one of my favorite games has become intolerable. <br><br>Am I the only one that's been wronged in such a way? I think not--and I ask you GameFan readers to send your stories to me (jtorres@gamefan.com). I want to hear stories like mine, stories of extreme cases in which the game's obviously wronged you,<br>tales of grave errors, unpleasant play experiences and GMs who just don't care. In fact,screen shots (small .jpg's only) of proven GMs saying particularly interesting things are encouraged, and I'll award the two best shots with a prize (I don't know what yet, but I'll find some game or T-shirt or something). I'll follow up this feature with some screen shots of what the GM said to me, as well as the best stories from you readers... Want to get your EQ problem off your chest and be published on one of the largest online gaming<br>sites? Now's your chance, GameFan readers--let the hard-core gamers come together and make a bold statement!<br><br>"We're not gonna take it...NO! We ain't gonna take it... We're not gonna take it anymore!"<br><br>--the immortal Twisted Sister<br><br>- Jasen Torres</font> <br><br>It's very much like many stories I have read over the last few months, but this one comes from a respected game site. Not from some disgruntled player (nothing against disgruntled players, I too am one).<br><br>There isn't really much else I can add to this. We all know how EQ GameMasters are, even if Verant doesn't. <br><br><font color="yellow">*worthless butts in*</font><br>Well... I agree that both of those instances should NOT happen in a non beta environment... but the fact is there are always gonna be bugs, no matter what the game. Losing things really sucks, especially when they were lost unjustly, BUT, it's also true that most of these circumstances can't be proven... Now, if Verant were to change policy and help people that cannot provide proof of their loss, well, what's stopping people from lieing and cheating to get things? Everyone that's played Diablo knows how bad cheating can ruin a game. Just months after release it was almost impossible to play Diablo online without also cheating. That could easily happen in EQ... I do believe that a majority of players are honest and trustworthy, BUT, camping for items and what not tends to make people behave strangely. Grown adults are bickering like children over spawn areas... Hell, I've even heard of people calling in sick to work to continue their 48 hour camping binge. And once these people get fed up with camping, or fed up with spawn point competition... what would stop them from petitioning to say they lost the item in question, and getting one for free? There would be no proof, so where would Verant draw the line over who gets an item and who doesn't? They would either have to give everyone what they ask for, or give nothing. While it does majorly suck that they give nothing, I would rather suffer through bugs than have to cheat to stay competitive. And before anyone says anything, yes, I have lost quite a bit to bugs. I've died many times to bugs. Sure I was pissed at the time, still am as a matter of fact... but I understand why they couldn't reimburse...<br><br><a href="http://www.eqrants.net/ubb/Forum2/HTML/000008.html"><b>Just in case you want to talk about this...</b></a></p>
<a name="newsitem961613482,23916,"></a>
<p><font face="Arial" size="4" color="#4E657C">Well Duh....</font> </strong><small>Posted Wednesday, June 21, 2000 by <a href="mailto:buur@eqrants.net">Buur</a></small><br><font face="Arial" size="3">I just saw this pretty funny e-mail that was written to the staff at <a href="http://eq.stratics.com">EQ Stratics</a>.<br><br><font color="#FFFF00">I had thought you guys have received your news, but I had a suspicison your news editors were taking news from other websites. <br><br>Today just confirmed it when I saw that someone put up news from caster's realm when all they had to do is get it themselves. Why bother with going to you guy's site for news when I can get it directly from the source like Caster's Realm, Everlore, and I dont know about vault.. They have crappy news..</font> <br><br>That's frickin' hilarious. This guy points out to Stratics that all the major, Verant friendly, news sites post almost the same exact news. He calls Casters Realm and Everlore sources? Hell, if it wasn't for Abashi posting on the EQ Bitchfest Casters Realm wouldn't have any news at all! Everlore is a little better, but still very heavy on the Abashi reliance. <br><br>I might not have the best content in the world, and lord knows I rarely post useful news, but at least I am not unoriginal.<br><br><font color="yellow">*worthless sticks his nose in other peoples business*</font><br>Well... this isn't the first time stratics has been caught stealing content from other sites... I remember a certain controversial UO site that released some info about t2a before it was released. Stratics ended up posting the pictures and even complete paragraphs stolen from said site. When contacted about the stolen content they claimed that the pictures and text had been sent in by their readers, and that unless said site produced proof of ownership, the pictures would not be removed. The least they coulda done was linked to this other mysterious site hehe...</p>
<a name="newsitem961589471,9886,"></a>
<p><font face="Arial" size="4" color="#4E657C">Wipe Out</font> </strong><small>Posted Wednesday, June 21, 2000 by <a href="mailto:buur@eqrants.net">Buur</a></small><br><font face="Arial" size="3">Unless are are living in a hole (not to be confused with the new zone, The Hole. Coming soon to an EQ server near you.) you know that Verant wiped out the test server. Many players play on that server exclusively. With the lower population you don't have to camp as long and you get to be really cool saying you play "The Test Server". A lot of players had months and months put into the test server, only to see all their hard work wiped out. Some are <a href="http://boards.station.sony.com/everquest/Forum4/HTML/054133.html">canceling their accounts</a> because they are kind of upset over this. Brad, you know him better as Aradune, McQuaid, had this to say.<br><br><font color="#FFFF00">Despite the Pwipe, more than 95% of the average population on the test server were playing tonight anyway.<br>They were there, starting anew, despite the huge time loss due to the PWipe.<br><br>We also received a lot of heartfelt emails (and very few flames).<br><br>So, after discussing this issue with 180 or so players on Test tonight, we've decided to seriously consider the following 2 options:<br><br>1. Do an item wipe on Test (i.e. restore the characters but without items).<br>2. Move on a voluntary basis characters from Test to Live servers. The characters would NOT go across with items.<br><br>It is also possible we will do both.<br><br>I got a lot of good feedback and passionate opinions and responses tonight, and I really appreciate it. The spirit of the vast majority of the players on Test is truly amazing and I admire you all.<br><br>While a total player wipe with select individuals being buffed to test mid and higher level zones might be clinically best for the Test Server, I think it's more fair to the players and testers there to do one or both of the options above.<br><br>In closing, if we choose option 2 (or both 1 and 2) I would really encourage those players on Test who are there more for playing and less for testing to seriously consider moving.<br><br>Another wipe, player or item, is not necessarily unlikely some time in the future.<br><br>Your comments and feedback are welcome,<br><br>-Brad</font><br> <br>That could end up being a decent resolution to a messy situation. <br><br>The problem I have with players getting upset about the wipe is that they knew it was a test server. That being so, you have to expect Verant to want to, you know, test on it. If they deem that the server needs to be wiped, then they are going to do just that. It is the test server, afterall. <br><br>On the other hand, the test server has been live for this long with no wipe. Of course the players that play there are going to form a community. They are part of a little group, the "Test Group". With very little warning Verant just swoops in and says, "Sorry, we are wiping your server. Start over and have a nice day." These are paying customers Verant. They actually paid their $10.00 a month to test your product. Actually I think we all pay $10.00 a month to test your product, it's just that most of us do it on the live servers under the guise of not being a test server.<br><br>I am not trying to walk on both sides of the fence here, just trying to show both sides of this issue. Someone actually used the message board and started a thread on this already, feel free to continue the discussion <a href="http://www.eqrants.net/ubb/Forum1/HTML/000003.html"><b>here</b></a>. </p>
<a name="newsitem961509897,98754,"></a>
<p><font face="Arial" size="4" color="#4E657C">E-Mail Scam</font> </strong><small>Posted Tuesday, June 20, 2000 by <a href="mailto:buur@eqrants.net">Buur</a></small><br><font face="Arial" size="3">There is apparently an e-mail scam going around. Someone out there wants your Station account name and password.<br><br><font color="#FFFF00">From: gordon_verantinteractive@hotmail.com <br>To: **********<br><br>Greetings,<br><br>We at Verant are doing a test. It's a Third-Party-Program test on all <br>accounts. We would like your Station name and password to test your account for use of these Third-Party-Programs. The information will be not be held after this test and will NOT be given to anyone. We are starting server by server on this test and will run over the course of the year. I thank you for your cooperation in these difficult times. If you dont know already a Third-Party-Program is a "cheat" used to "hack" Everquest by using a "outside" program to confuse the servers. Once again i would like to thank you for your help.<br><br>Sincerly,<br>Gordon<br>Verant Interactive<br></font> <br><br>Let me just go out on a limb here and say this, if you fall for this you are the dumbest individual on the planet.</p>
