<p><font face="Arial" size="4" color="#4E657C">EQ for the P2 revisited...</font> </strong><small>Posted Tuesday, June 13, 2000 by <a href="mailto:holychit@hotmail.com">worthless</a></small><br><font face="Arial" size="3">I've been giving this a little thought... Verant wouldn't need to hire artists for a P2 eq port, cause even though the coding may change, I am sure they can import all the art files. So their big console game is probably not EQ... One of their other projects, Sovereign has already been shown at E3, so it is pretty certain this will be released on PC... that leaves Star Wars Online as the only other announced project not ruled out thus far.<br><br><a href="http://www.theforce.net" target="_blank">Theforce.net</a> recently received an anonymous email from a supposed person close to LucasArts who claims to have seen an early build of a PC based Star Wars Online... Mr. Anonymous claims that Darth Maul-like characters are in the game, <a href="http://www.solosnet.com/tourney/refs.cfm?site=61" target="_blank">BUT</a> the game is supposed to be based on the original trilogy, in which there was no mention of Darth Maul-like creatures(that I know of, I am a total Star Wars geek, but I haven't read ALL of the books). To me this sounds a bit fishy... and the fact that the anonymous mailer really had no other info about the gameplay design doesn't give me much faith in his story. Besides, it's still very early in the game's development, I doubt they would have any kind of working build yet. If the PC build story is unfounded, this leaves open the possibility that Sony may have other plans for Star Wars Online...<br><br>As you read here last week, Sony has just purchased Verant. They now have full say in what Verant does, and Verant games are being made to be marketed exclusively by Sony, the makers of the Playstation 2. The initial shipment of PS2 systems, due in October, will definitely sell out almost immediately. Supposedly, if you don't get one of these first systems you shouldn't expect to get one till 2001. Even if Sony does ship more systems, the Christmas demand will keep the shelves empty for awhile. But, later in 2001, when the initial "new console hype" dies down and the next gen Nintendo system is on the verge of being released, Sony will need a way to insure that their console remains the top seller. Could this be where Star Wars Online fits in? <br><br>Now this is all speculation of course, but what better way to sell a million PS2's than make SWO a PS2 only game? Star Wars Online isn't do for release till late 2001, just in time for another Christmas buying season. A perfect opportunity for Sony to reinstate the PS2 hype with a game based on the biggest movie license of all time. I could very well be wrong (and actually, I hope that I am), but it seems to me that we PC users will be shelling out quite a bit if we want to get SWO up and running...<br><br>Dammit, I knew I shoulda waited to buy a DVD drive!</p>
